// TAS-130: the Increment 2 unsafe narrow passing variant.
//
// The same shared bikeway and the same `pass` tactic as `narrow_passing_v2`,
// with the two authored differences that make the executed pass a close one: the
// bikeway is 3.6 m wide and both modes author a 0.4 m
// `lateral.target_clearance_m`. Together they put the executed pass at 0.4001 m
// of body-to-body clearance, inside the 0.5 m band this fixture's jurisdiction
// marks as a violation and above both the mode's own `lateral_clearance_m`
// (0.3 m) and the commit policy's `min_predicted_clearance_m` (0.25 m), which are
// untouched: the maneuver stays admissible and completes. The width is what puts
// the pass inside the band — the same 0.4 m target on `narrow_passing_v2`'s 6.0 m
// bikeway executes at 0.7786 m, which no band records.
//
// The point of the variant is the documented *violation*, not a new tolerance:
// `docs/schema-v2-contract.md` *`clearance_bands[]`* fixes that a band with
// `violation: true` records a lateral-displacement violation when the observed
// minimum clearance falls below its threshold, and *Metrics and output* fixes
// `close_pass_violations` as the family that counts those observations. The
// bands below are the same two `narrow_passing_v2` declares — the same 0.5 m
// violation threshold and the same 2.0 m study band — so the difference between
// the two fixtures is the pass that was executed, not the rule it is judged by:
// the safe fixture's pass stays above the threshold with no violation, and this
// fixture's pass falls inside it and is recorded as one.
//
// Measured at the pinned benchmark seed (0) and step (0.05 s): the bicycle
// (9.0 m/s) catches the 4.5 m/s scooter, the run carries exactly one overtaking
// interval (ticks 895..925), its minimum clearance is 0.4001 m at 44.75 s with a
// relative speed of 0.457 m/s, the violation band (index 0) is recorded, both
// declared bands accumulate 1.55 s, no contact or overlap is recorded, and the
// executed pass engages the anti-overlap position cap for exactly one step — the
// same one-cap-per-pass relationship the safe fixture and the landed
// `narrow_passing.rs` model fixture report. Both demand rates are authored
// sparse exactly as the safe fixture's are, so the run carries one leader and
// one follower and one interval.
//
// This is Increment 2's close-pass violation evidence for `CC-OVERTAKE`; it is
// not the cell's `T-O2` reference pass. That is
// `scenarios/phase2/inc2/narrow_passing_v2.json5`, whose executed pass stays at
// or above the authored band threshold.
